1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:10,722
Last time we built one step out of nothing but matrix products.

2
00:00:10,722 --> 00:00:17,432
Three halves W, minus one half W, W transpose, W.

3
00:00:17,532 --> 00:00:21,549
On each singular value, that step is a polynomial.

4
00:00:21,549 --> 00:00:26,450
p of sigma is three halves sigma, minus one half sigma cubed.

5
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:32,542
We chose it so that one stays at one, and a value close to one moves closer.

6
00:00:32,642 --> 00:00:36,142
But a real sigma can start anywhere.

7
00:00:38,183 --> 00:00:40,125
Where does a sigma end up?

8
00:00:40,125 --> 00:00:41,727
No theory yet.

9
00:00:41,727 --> 00:00:46,521
Let's just pick a number and try it, say zero point five.

10
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:52,362
One step of p takes three halves of the number, and subtracts one half of its cube.

11
00:00:52,362 --> 00:00:56,496
Three halves of zero point five is zero point seven five.

12
00:00:56,496 --> 00:01:00,232
Zero point five cubed is zero point one two five,

13
00:01:00,232 --> 00:01:03,968
and half of that is zero point zero six two five.

14
00:01:03,968 --> 00:01:09,012
Subtract, and we get zero point six eight seven five.

15
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:12,780
Now feed that result back in, and keep going.

16
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:18,677
Zero point six nine becomes zero point eight seven, then zero point nine eight,

17
00:01:18,677 --> 00:01:20,992
then zero point nine nine nine.

18
00:01:20,992 --> 00:01:23,794
It is closing in on one.

19
00:01:24,216 --> 00:01:26,248
That start was below one.

20
00:01:26,248 --> 00:01:27,980
Now try one above it.

21
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:32,653
One point three drops to zero point eight five, which is below one,

22
00:01:32,653 --> 00:01:38,372
and then it climbs, to zero point nine seven, and then zero point nine nine eight.

23
00:01:38,372 --> 00:01:41,362
It also closes in on one.

24
00:01:41,783 --> 00:01:44,413
Both of those were fairly close to one.

25
00:01:44,413 --> 00:01:48,187
So try a start that is far away, down near zero.

26
00:01:48,187 --> 00:01:53,566
Zero point one becomes zero point one five, then zero point two two,

27
00:01:53,566 --> 00:01:55,781
then zero point three three.

28
00:01:55,781 --> 00:01:59,130
It is slow at first, but it keeps climbing,

29
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:02,401
and after eight steps it also reaches one.

30
00:02:02,850 --> 00:02:05,346
All three starts end at one.

31
00:02:05,346 --> 00:02:08,788
And once a value is at one, it stays there.

32
00:02:08,788 --> 00:02:13,676
Three halves minus one half is one, so p of one equals one.

33
00:02:13,676 --> 00:02:16,734
That is our first wish at work.

34
00:02:17,183 --> 00:02:19,892
But if some other number also stayed put,

35
00:02:19,892 --> 00:02:23,197
a sigma could get stuck there and never reach one.

36
00:02:23,197 --> 00:02:24,909
Is there such a number?

37
00:02:24,909 --> 00:02:26,441
Try zero.

38
00:02:26,441 --> 00:02:32,915
Three halves of zero is zero, and zero cubed is zero, so p of zero is zero.

39
00:02:32,915 --> 00:02:35,717
Zero stays put as well.

40
00:02:35,817 --> 00:02:38,695
From here on, call the input x.

41
00:02:38,695 --> 00:02:42,895
A value where p of x equals x is called a fixed point.

42
00:02:42,895 --> 00:02:44,677
We have found two of them.

43
00:02:44,677 --> 00:02:46,979
Are there any more?

44
00:02:47,416 --> 00:02:51,836
To find them all at once, write down p of x equals x.

45
00:02:51,836 --> 00:02:57,134
That is three halves x, minus one half x cubed, equals x.

46
00:02:57,134 --> 00:03:04,140
Subtract x from both sides, and we get one half x, minus one half x cubed,

47
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:05,276
equals zero.

48
00:03:05,716 --> 00:03:09,430
Both terms contain one half x, so pull it out.

49
00:03:09,430 --> 00:03:12,756
What is left inside is one minus x squared.

50
00:03:12,756 --> 00:03:18,602
And one minus x squared splits into one minus x, times one plus x.

51
00:03:18,602 --> 00:03:26,682
So the equation says one half x, times one minus x, times one plus x, equals zero.

52
00:03:27,116 --> 00:03:31,332
A product is zero only if one of its factors is zero.

53
00:03:31,332 --> 00:03:36,132
So x is zero, or x is one, or x is minus one.

54
00:03:36,132 --> 00:03:41,010
Those are all the fixed points there are, the two we found and a new one.

55
00:03:41,010 --> 00:03:43,314
And minus one does check out.

56
00:03:43,314 --> 00:03:48,268
Minus three halves, plus one half, is minus one.

57
00:03:48,368 --> 00:03:51,203
A singular value is never negative,

58
00:03:51,203 --> 00:03:55,172
so for now minus one is just a point on the list.

59
00:03:55,616 --> 00:03:57,906
But zero matters right away.

60
00:03:57,906 --> 00:04:01,026
A sigma at exactly zero is stuck.

61
00:04:01,026 --> 00:04:07,260
And yet zero point one, right next to zero, walked away from it, all the way to one.

62
00:04:07,260 --> 00:04:11,672
Why would one fixed point pull values in and another push them away?

63
00:04:11,672 --> 00:04:16,934
To see what happens around each of them, we need a picture of the iteration.

64
00:04:18,183 --> 00:04:20,439
Start with the graph of p.

65
00:04:20,439 --> 00:04:26,646
Along the bottom is the input x, and the height of the curve above it is the output,

66
00:04:26,646 --> 00:04:27,163
p of x.

67
00:04:27,163 --> 00:04:29,155
We know a few points already.

68
00:04:29,155 --> 00:04:33,753
At zero the height is zero, and at one the height is one.

69
00:04:33,753 --> 00:04:40,163
The curve rises from zero, reaches a hump at one, and comes back down after it.

70
00:04:40,616 --> 00:04:44,652
On this picture, one step of the iteration looks like this.

71
00:04:44,652 --> 00:04:48,535
Start at x equals zero point three on the bottom axis,

72
00:04:48,535 --> 00:04:50,836
and go straight up to the curve.

73
00:04:50,836 --> 00:04:56,900
The height you reach is p of zero point three, about zero point four four.

74
00:04:57,350 --> 00:05:02,048
For the next step, zero point four four has to become the new input.

75
00:05:02,048 --> 00:05:06,338
But right now it is a height, and inputs are measured along the bottom.

76
00:05:06,338 --> 00:05:10,272
We need a way to turn a height into a position.

77
00:05:10,716 --> 00:05:15,652
Here is a line that does exactly that, the diagonal y equals x.

78
00:05:15,652 --> 00:05:19,450
Every point on it is as far to the right as it is high.

79
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:23,160
So go sideways from the curve until you hit the diagonal.

80
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:26,424
You are still at height zero point four four,

81
00:05:26,424 --> 00:05:30,558
and now you are also above x equals zero point four four.

82
00:05:31,016 --> 00:05:34,406
From there, the second step is the same move.

83
00:05:34,406 --> 00:05:38,540
Go straight up to the curve, which gives zero point six one,

84
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:40,952
and sideways to the diagonal again.

85
00:05:40,952 --> 00:05:46,717
Keep repeating, and the path climbs like a staircase, zero point eight,

86
00:05:46,717 --> 00:05:49,558
zero point nine five, and into one.

87
00:05:50,016 --> 00:05:51,918
Now a start above one.

88
00:05:51,918 --> 00:05:57,855
From one point two, the curve is below the diagonal, so the first move goes down,

89
00:05:57,855 --> 00:05:59,614
to zero point nine four.

90
00:05:59,614 --> 00:06:05,332
After that the path climbs the last little bit and settles on one as well.

91
00:06:05,783 --> 00:06:08,903
Now look at where the curve meets the diagonal.

92
00:06:08,903 --> 00:06:14,691
On the diagonal the height equals x, and on the curve the height is p of x.

93
00:06:14,691 --> 00:06:18,103
So at a crossing, p of x equals x.

94
00:06:18,103 --> 00:06:24,659
The three crossings are our three fixed points, minus one, zero, and one.

95
00:06:24,759 --> 00:06:28,241
And the staircases show which way things move around them.

96
00:06:28,241 --> 00:06:33,403
Near zero the path walks away, and near one it walks in.

97
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:39,132
Why does one pull values in, while zero pushes them away?

98
00:06:39,132 --> 00:06:41,560
For one, we already know.

99
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:45,836
When we built p, we put in one plus a small error e,

100
00:06:45,836 --> 00:06:50,936
and we chose p so that one step multiplies that error by zero.

101
00:06:51,383 --> 00:06:53,615
So do the same thing at zero.

102
00:06:53,615 --> 00:06:57,129
A value near zero is just a small error e.

103
00:06:57,129 --> 00:07:02,983
Put it into p, and we get one point five e, minus one half e cubed.

104
00:07:02,983 --> 00:07:06,043
For a small e, the cube is tiny.

105
00:07:06,043 --> 00:07:11,961
If e is zero point one, e cubed is only zero point zero zero one.

106
00:07:11,961 --> 00:07:15,477
So p gives about one point five e.

107
00:07:15,577 --> 00:07:22,307
This time, one step multiplies the error by one point five, and the error grows.

108
00:07:22,750 --> 00:07:24,982
We have seen this growth already.

109
00:07:24,982 --> 00:07:30,334
Zero point one went to zero point one five, then zero point two two,

110
00:07:30,334 --> 00:07:32,538
then zero point three three.

111
00:07:32,538 --> 00:07:36,464
Each value is about one and a half times the one before.

112
00:07:36,464 --> 00:07:40,216
That is why zero pushes values away.

113
00:07:40,316 --> 00:07:44,302
On the graph, this multiplier is the slope of the curve.

114
00:07:44,302 --> 00:07:48,218
Zoom in at zero, and the curve looks like a straight line.

115
00:07:48,218 --> 00:07:52,836
Move e to the right, and it rises by one point five e.

116
00:07:52,836 --> 00:07:56,368
Rise over run is one point five.

117
00:07:56,816 --> 00:07:58,478
So here is the rule.

118
00:07:58,478 --> 00:08:03,922
Near a fixed point, each step multiplies the error by the slope of the curve there.

119
00:08:03,922 --> 00:08:09,444
If the slope is bigger than one in size, the error grows and values are pushed away.

120
00:08:09,444 --> 00:08:14,926
If it is smaller than one, the error shrinks and values are pulled in.

121
00:08:15,026 --> 00:08:18,017
At zero the slope is one point five,

122
00:08:18,017 --> 00:08:23,169
and zero point zero five drifts to zero point zero seven five,

123
00:08:23,169 --> 00:08:25,828
then zero point one one, and on.

124
00:08:26,283 --> 00:08:31,233
At one, the multiplier is zero, so the slope is zero.

125
00:08:31,233 --> 00:08:34,011
The curve is flat at the top of its hump.

126
00:08:34,011 --> 00:08:37,871
That is our second wish, seen as a picture.

127
00:08:37,971 --> 00:08:41,639
But an error cannot vanish completely in one step.

128
00:08:41,639 --> 00:08:46,196
When we built p, we dropped the terms with e squared and e cubed,

129
00:08:46,196 --> 00:08:47,879
because they were small.

130
00:08:47,879 --> 00:08:50,281
Now put them back.

131
00:08:50,381 --> 00:08:54,172
p of one plus e is three halves times one plus e,

132
00:08:54,172 --> 00:08:56,879
minus one half times the full cube,

133
00:08:56,879 --> 00:09:00,825
one plus three e plus three e squared plus e cubed.

134
00:09:00,825 --> 00:09:02,777
The ones add up to one.

135
00:09:02,777 --> 00:09:06,023
The terms with e cancel, as we arranged.

136
00:09:06,023 --> 00:09:12,253
What is left is minus one and a half e squared, minus one half e cubed.

137
00:09:12,353 --> 00:09:16,807
So the new error is about minus one and a half times e squared.

138
00:09:16,807 --> 00:09:23,215
The minus sign says that we land just below one, whichever side we started on.

139
00:09:23,315 --> 00:09:25,307
Start at one point two.

140
00:09:25,307 --> 00:09:30,209
The error goes from zero point two, to zero point zero six,

141
00:09:30,209 --> 00:09:32,535
to zero point zero zero six.

142
00:09:32,535 --> 00:09:38,213
Each step roughly squares it, which is far faster than shrinking by a fixed factor.

143
00:09:38,213 --> 00:09:42,953
The same calculation at minus one gives the same result.

144
00:09:43,416 --> 00:09:46,988
So zero is unstable, like the top of a hill,

145
00:09:46,988 --> 00:09:50,804
where the smallest push sends you rolling away.

146
00:09:50,804 --> 00:09:55,803
One and minus one are stable, like the bottoms of two valleys,

147
00:09:55,803 --> 00:09:58,464
where everything nearby rolls in.

148
00:09:59,883 --> 00:10:03,429
So far every start rolled into the valley at one.

149
00:10:03,429 --> 00:10:06,615
But the largest start we tried was one point three,

150
00:10:06,615 --> 00:10:08,677
and we don't get to choose sigma.

151
00:10:08,677 --> 00:10:12,709
How large can it be before something goes wrong?

152
00:10:13,150 --> 00:10:15,976
Go a little bigger, to one point five.

153
00:10:15,976 --> 00:10:20,164
That is past the hump, where the curve is already coming down,

154
00:10:20,164 --> 00:10:24,150
so the first step drops all the way to zero point five six.

155
00:10:24,150 --> 00:10:30,155
But from there it climbs as before, zero point seven five, zero point nine two,

156
00:10:30,155 --> 00:10:31,144
and into one.

157
00:10:31,144 --> 00:10:33,346
Still fine.

158
00:10:33,783 --> 00:10:36,523
A little bigger again, one point eight.

159
00:10:36,523 --> 00:10:40,107
Before we look, where do you think it ends up?

160
00:10:42,050 --> 00:10:43,372
Do the step.

161
00:10:43,372 --> 00:10:46,854
Three halves of one point eight is two point seven.

162
00:10:46,854 --> 00:10:51,106
But half of its cube is two point nine two, which is bigger.

163
00:10:51,106 --> 00:10:57,452
Subtract, and the result is negative, minus zero point two one six.

164
00:10:57,552 --> 00:11:01,546
What does p do with a negative number? p has only odd powers,

165
00:11:01,546 --> 00:11:06,000
so flipping the sign of the input just flips the sign of the output.

166
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,932
Plus zero point two one six goes to plus zero point three two,

167
00:11:10,932 --> 00:11:16,262
so minus zero point two one six goes to minus zero point three two.

168
00:11:16,362 --> 00:11:20,254
The negative side is a mirror image of the positive side.

169
00:11:20,254 --> 00:11:22,270
Call this the mirror rule.

170
00:11:22,270 --> 00:11:27,064
So this value does what its mirror image would do, with the sign flipped.

171
00:11:27,064 --> 00:11:31,702
It moves away from zero and settles at minus one.

172
00:11:31,802 --> 00:11:36,480
For a singular value, that has the right size but the wrong sign.

173
00:11:36,480 --> 00:11:39,794
This direction ends up reversed.

174
00:11:41,216 --> 00:11:46,294
So what went wrong was the very first step, which landed below the axis.

175
00:11:46,294 --> 00:11:50,320
On the graph, that is where the curve, after its hump,

176
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:52,706
comes down and crosses the axis.

177
00:11:52,706 --> 00:11:56,688
Past that crossing, p of x is negative.

178
00:11:59,716 --> 00:12:01,378
Where is the crossing?

179
00:12:01,378 --> 00:12:04,662
Both terms of p contain x over two.

180
00:12:04,662 --> 00:12:07,988
Three halves x is x over two times three,

181
00:12:07,988 --> 00:12:12,124
and one half x cubed is x over two times x squared.

182
00:12:12,124 --> 00:12:17,952
So p of x is x over two, times three minus x squared.

183
00:12:18,383 --> 00:12:23,601
For a positive x, the first factor, x over two, is positive.

184
00:12:23,601 --> 00:12:29,261
So the sign of p comes from the second factor, three minus x squared.

185
00:12:29,683 --> 00:12:34,532
Three minus x squared is positive while x squared is below three,

186
00:12:34,532 --> 00:12:37,591
and negative once x squared passes three.

187
00:12:37,591 --> 00:12:44,141
The change happens at x equals square root of three, about one point seven three.

188
00:12:47,183 --> 00:12:51,545
One point five is below square root of three, and it was fine.

189
00:12:51,545 --> 00:12:54,841
One point eight is just above it, and it flipped.

190
00:12:54,841 --> 00:12:57,131
That matches.

191
00:12:57,583 --> 00:13:01,373
Then is every start below square root of three safe?

192
00:13:01,373 --> 00:13:05,257
Look at the curve between zero and square root of three.

193
00:13:05,257 --> 00:13:09,964
It stays above the axis, and its highest point, the top of the hump,

194
00:13:09,964 --> 00:13:11,141
is at height one.

195
00:13:11,141 --> 00:13:13,723
So wherever we start in this range,

196
00:13:13,723 --> 00:13:17,559
the first step lands somewhere between zero and one.

197
00:13:18,016 --> 00:13:22,576
And between zero and one, the curve sits above the diagonal.

198
00:13:22,576 --> 00:13:27,500
That means the output is bigger than the input, so every step climbs.

199
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:32,294
It cannot climb past one, because the curve never goes higher than one.

200
00:13:32,294 --> 00:13:37,440
A value that keeps climbing and can never pass one has to settle somewhere.

201
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:42,918
And the only place where it can settle is a fixed point, so it ends at one.

202
00:13:43,350 --> 00:13:44,732
So yes.

203
00:13:44,732 --> 00:13:50,378
Every sigma between zero and square root of three ends at one.

204
00:13:50,816 --> 00:13:56,994
And exactly at square root of three, the second factor is zero, so p gives zero.

205
00:13:56,994 --> 00:14:02,414
The value lands on the fixed point at zero, and it stays there forever.

206
00:14:03,850 --> 00:14:08,572
So past square root of three, the first step flips the sign.

207
00:14:08,572 --> 00:14:12,546
One point eight flipped and still settled, at minus one.

208
00:14:12,546 --> 00:14:15,458
Maybe a flip is all that ever happens.

209
00:14:15,458 --> 00:14:19,252
Test that with a much bigger start, three.

210
00:14:19,683 --> 00:14:22,443
Three halves of three is four point five,

211
00:14:22,443 --> 00:14:25,607
and half of three cubed is thirteen point five.

212
00:14:25,607 --> 00:14:28,385
Subtract, and we get minus nine.

213
00:14:28,385 --> 00:14:30,507
It flipped, as expected.

214
00:14:30,507 --> 00:14:32,079
Now the next step.

215
00:14:32,079 --> 00:14:37,555
By the mirror rule, minus nine does what nine does, with the sign flipped.

216
00:14:37,555 --> 00:14:42,554
Nine goes to thirteen point five minus three hundred sixty four point five,

217
00:14:42,554 --> 00:14:45,153
which is minus three hundred fifty one.

218
00:14:45,153 --> 00:14:49,627
So minus nine goes to plus three hundred fifty one.

219
00:14:49,727 --> 00:14:53,801
It flips every time, and it gets bigger every time.

220
00:14:53,801 --> 00:14:56,473
This one explodes.

221
00:14:56,916 --> 00:15:01,931
Both one point eight and three flip, so the difference must be in the size,

222
00:15:01,931 --> 00:15:04,338
meaning the number without its sign.

223
00:15:04,338 --> 00:15:09,212
One point eight came out at size zero point two, smaller than it went in.

224
00:15:09,212 --> 00:15:13,394
Three came out at size nine, bigger than it went in.

225
00:15:13,494 --> 00:15:17,370
So somewhere between them, shrinking turns into growing.

226
00:15:17,370 --> 00:15:18,942
Narrow it down.

227
00:15:18,942 --> 00:15:23,338
Two point zero goes to minus one, so its size is one.

228
00:15:23,338 --> 00:15:25,930
That is still smaller.

229
00:15:26,350 --> 00:15:30,166
But two point three goes to minus two point six three.

230
00:15:30,166 --> 00:15:32,844
Its size is bigger than where it started.

231
00:15:32,844 --> 00:15:38,438
So the turning point is somewhere between two point zero and two point three.

232
00:15:39,883 --> 00:15:42,551
Where exactly is the turning point?

233
00:15:42,551 --> 00:15:46,089
Compare the size after a step with the size before.

234
00:15:46,089 --> 00:15:53,257
Use the factored form. p of x is x over two, times three minus x squared.

235
00:15:53,357 --> 00:15:59,877
So the size of p of x is the size of x, times the size of three minus x squared,

236
00:15:59,877 --> 00:16:00,611
over two.

237
00:16:00,611 --> 00:16:07,049
Past square root of three, that second part is x squared minus three, over two.

238
00:16:07,049 --> 00:16:09,041
Call it the size factor.

239
00:16:09,041 --> 00:16:13,305
Each step multiplies the size by this factor.

240
00:16:13,750 --> 00:16:15,996
Check it with the starts we tried.

241
00:16:15,996 --> 00:16:22,024
At two point zero, the factor is four minus three, over two, which is one half.

242
00:16:22,024 --> 00:16:24,156
And two did go to size one.

243
00:16:24,156 --> 00:16:28,436
At two point three, the factor is about one point one four,

244
00:16:28,436 --> 00:16:31,338
and two point three did come out bigger.

245
00:16:31,438 --> 00:16:35,018
So a factor below one means flip and shrink.

246
00:16:35,018 --> 00:16:38,856
A factor above one means flip and grow.

247
00:16:39,316 --> 00:16:43,212
The turning point is where the factor is exactly one.

248
00:16:43,212 --> 00:16:49,382
That means x squared minus three equals two, so x squared equals five.

249
00:16:49,382 --> 00:16:53,778
The turning point is square root of five, about two point two four,

250
00:16:53,778 --> 00:16:57,648
and it does sit between two point zero and two point three.

251
00:16:57,648 --> 00:16:58,607
On the curve,

252
00:16:58,607 --> 00:17:04,285
between square root of three and square root of five a step shrinks the size,

253
00:17:04,285 --> 00:17:07,234
and beyond square root of five it grows.

254
00:17:10,483 --> 00:17:13,639
What happens exactly at square root of five?

255
00:17:13,639 --> 00:17:19,883
Use the factored form. x over two, times three minus five, is minus x.

256
00:17:19,883 --> 00:17:23,903
So square root of five goes to minus square root of five.

257
00:17:23,903 --> 00:17:28,749
By the mirror rule, that goes straight back to plus square root of five.

258
00:17:28,749 --> 00:17:32,797
On the picture, the path becomes a square.

259
00:17:33,250 --> 00:17:37,632
So square root of five neither settles nor explodes.

260
00:17:37,632 --> 00:17:42,922
It jumps back and forth between plus and minus square root of five forever.

261
00:17:42,922 --> 00:17:48,840
This is called a period two orbit, because it repeats every two steps.

262
00:17:50,283 --> 00:17:54,881
And past square root of five, every step flips and grows.

263
00:17:54,881 --> 00:18:00,226
Two point three goes to minus two point six three, then five point one eight,

264
00:18:00,226 --> 00:18:02,517
then minus sixty one point eight.

265
00:18:02,517 --> 00:18:05,503
Like three, it explodes.

266
00:18:07,250 --> 00:18:09,702
So now we have two thresholds.

267
00:18:09,702 --> 00:18:13,810
Below square root of three, every start ends at one.

268
00:18:13,810 --> 00:18:18,596
Beyond square root of five, every start explodes.

269
00:18:18,696 --> 00:18:23,304
Between them there is a gap, and we have only tried two starts in it,

270
00:18:23,304 --> 00:18:25,642
one point eight and two point zero.

271
00:18:25,642 --> 00:18:27,724
Both ended at minus one.

272
00:18:27,724 --> 00:18:30,414
Does every start in the gap do that?

273
00:18:30,414 --> 00:18:32,766
That is next time.

